-\paragraph{\textbf{Limitations.}} Every comparison here is made under a linear readout, which is a
-choice rather than a concession: logistic regression over named axes is cheap, auditable, and stable
-enough that a change in the data appears in the coefficients rather than in the optimiser. We claim
-complementarity in that setting, not for readouts of arbitrary capacity.
-Our corpus is the bright ($T < 10$) two-minute sample, which is
-better studied and less confused than the faint population a survey-scale claim would need. Every arm
-sees only each star's first segment ($\approx 5.7$\,d), which equalises access but bounds the
-supervised baselines at the probe's input scope rather than in general. Our labels come from incomplete catalogues, so a
-model-positive, catalogue-negative star scores as a false positive even when genuine. Rotation-period regression saturates at the 5.69-day input
-baseline -- stars with true periods averaging 8.60 and 17.34 days are both answered near 5.6 -- so its
-$R^{2}$ is not period inference beyond the segment length. Flare is an activity label, not a detection
-label: 81\% of labelled positives show no catalogued flare inside the encoded segment, so we claim
-flare propensity only; auditing its negative class against the detector's own published search cut
-moved the gain from $+0.0506$ to $+0.0507$. Under this frozen readout the advantage is also a
-large-label phenomenon, narrowing on 6 of 10 tasks as the labelled budget falls towards one percent.
+\paragraph{\textbf{Limitations and next steps.}} The four linear-readout arms (\texttt{features},
+$\mu$, \texttt{features}\,$\oplus\,\mu$, and the untrained control) share one linear model. It has few
+parameters, so it cannot rescue weak features, and it is identical across the four, so the comparison
+is between feature sets. Our claim is made for a linear readout, and we make no claim about more
+powerful readouts. Our corpus is the bright ($T < 10$) two-minute sample, and every arm, the two
+supervised baselines included, sees one 5.7-day segment per star. The fainter and far more numerous
+stars, and longer light curves spanning several TESS sectors, are the obvious next experiments; the
+latter would let rotation periods longer than 5.7 days be measured, which one segment cannot do. Our
+negatives are stars absent from the label catalogues, not stars verified to lack the signal, so a
+correct detection the catalogue missed counts against us; every detection score is therefore a lower
+bound. The flare label marks stars that have flared somewhere in their TESS data, not a flare inside
+the 5.7-day segment we encode, so the task is recognising a flaring star, not finding a flare. When
+the readout is trained on one percent of the labels, the advantage narrows on 6 of 10 tasks, so how
+much pre-training helps when labels are scarce remains an open question.
 
